    {UA_JSONKEY_TYPE, NULL, NULL, false, NULL},
    {UA_JSONKEY_BODY, &dst->data, (decodeJsonSignature) Array_decodeJson, false, NULL},
    ...
};
ret = decodeFields(ctx, parseCtx, entries, 2, bodyType);
```

`Array_decodeJson` is called with `bodyType` (ExtensionObject). It decodes an array of ExtensionObjects.

In `Array_decodeJson_internal`:
```c
size_t *p = (size_t*) dst - 1;
*p = length;
*dst = UA_calloc(length, type->memSize);
...
uintptr_t ptr = (uintptr_t)*dst;
for(size_t i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
    ret = decodeJsonJumpTable[type->typeKind]((void*)ptr, type, ctx, parseCtx, true);
    ...
    ptr += type->memSize;
}
```

`dst` is `&variant->data`. `(size_t*)dst - 1` is `&variant->arrayLength`. So `*p = length` sets `variant->arrayLength = length`.

Then `*dst = UA_calloc(length, type->memSize)` allocates memory for the array elements. `type->memSize` is the size of an ExtensionObject.

Then for each element, `decodeJsonJumpTable[UA_TYPES_EXTENSIONOBJECT]` is called, which is `ExtensionObject_decodeJson`.

In `ExtensionObject_decodeJson`, when no encoding is found (structure encoding):
```c
dst->content.decoded.data = UA_new(typeOfBody);
...
DecodeEntry entries[2] = {
    {UA_JSONKEY_TYPEID, &typeId_dummy, ...},
    {UA_JSONKEY_BODY, dst->content.decoded.data,
     (decodeJsonSignature) decodeJsonJumpTable[typeOfBody->typeKind], ...}
};
return decodeFields(ctx, parseCtx, entries, 2, typeOfBody);
```

This allocates memory for the decoded type and decodes the body into it.

When `Variant_clear` is called:
1. `p->type` is ExtensionObject
2. `p->data` is an array of ExtensionObjects
3. `p->arrayLength` is the number of elements
4. `UA_Array_delete(p->data, p->arrayLength, &UA_TYPES[UA_TYPES_EXTENSIONOBJECT])` is called
5. For each element, `ExtensionObject_clear` is called
6. If encoding is DECODED, `UA_delete(p->content.decoded.data, p->content.decoded.type)` is called
7. The decoded type's clear function is called

This should work correctly.

